## 7. Relationship to Operator's Existing Architecture

### 7.1 What Already Exists (and maps cleanly)

| Existing Component | Maps To |
|---|---|
| `.agent/` documentation scaffold | Constitution + scoped context delivery |
| Inter-agent handoff contracts with artifact schemas | Structured intermediate outputs (MetaGPT pattern) |
| Sanitized Project Profiles (two-tier privacy) | Constitution layer with redacted/full variants |
| Architect agent asking questions | Questioning Protocol (front-loading spec quality) |
| Designer agent asking questions | Same — spec refinement phase |
| Qualia-informed iterative reviews | Review Gate (outer loop cycle) |
| Scoped agent context (minimizing redundant reads) | Progressive disclosure / context management |
| Agents that ask rather than assume | Three-tier boundary model (Ask First tier) |

### 7.2 What's Missing (and should be added)

1. **Formal Frozen/Mutable Spec Document:** The operator likely has informal versions of this scattered across project docs. Consolidating into a single two-section artifact with explicit rules about who can modify what is the highest-impact change.

2. **Progress Ledger:** A standardized session-bridging document that all agents read at cycle start and update at cycle end. This is the mechanism that prevents "where were we?" confusion across context windows.

3. **Explicit Review Gate Protocol:** Formalizing the outer-loop review as a defined process step (not ad hoc) with a checklist: drift assessment, mutable spec review, next-batch generation.

4. **Conformance Checks:** Automated or semi-automated validation that implementation matches frozen spec acceptance criteria. Can be as simple as "agent compares output against spec checklist" or as robust as contract testing.

5. **Generalized Template:** A project-agnostic starter kit that can be instantiated for any new project in the operator's portfolio. The three artifacts (constitution, spec, ledger) with standard sections and the questioning protocol as a Phase 0 checklist.

